  "interpretation": {
    "main_finding": "The naive instance-level sign test becomes increasingly anti-conservative as within-family dependence increases under this declared null model. The family-median sign test maintains type-I error below 5% in every tested ICC cell.",
    "discreteness": "At 10 independent clusters, a one-sided exact sign test rejects at alpha=0.05 only with at least 9 cluster wins; its attainable type-I error is 11/1024 (about 1.07%), so the cluster result is deliberately conservative and may have low power.",
    "limitations": [
      "Only one balanced cluster-size design is used in the Monte Carlo ICC sweep",
      "The exchangeable two-point latent-probability model is not a universal model of benchmark dependence",
      "No power analysis, heterogeneous treatment effects or missingness stress is included here",
      "Simulation does not validate independence or representativeness of a real quantum benchmark suite"
    ]
  }
